\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A two-by-two paired expectation}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Take one retained pair and one eliminated pair, and let
##{K=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}}
in that order. The eliminated compression is #{D=1}, and the Schur complement is #{S=1}. Write #{a=\psi_R\bar\psi_R}. The partial formula gives the marginal weight #{z_R=1+a}. Direct expansion of the [normalized paired conditional functional](fdip-00L5) gives
##{\mathcal E_R^K(\psi_E)=-\psi_R,\qquad
\mathcal E_R^K(\bar\psi_E)=-\bar\psi_R,\qquad
\mathcal E_R^K(\psi_E\bar\psi_E)=1+a.}
The retained state has #{\omega_S(a)=1}, so
##{\omega_S\bigl(\mathcal E_R^K(\psi_E\bar\psi_E)\bigr)=2,}
the eliminated diagonal entry of #{K^{-1}}. The raw coefficient is the marginal weight #{J_E(e^{\theta_K})=1+a}. Dividing by #{\det D=1} leaves that weight. Multiplication by #{e^{-\theta_S}=1-a} is what gives #{\mathcal E_R^K(1)=1}.}

\p{The diagonal matrix #{\operatorname{diag}(0,1)} has invertible eliminated compression and Schur complement #{0}. Its normalized paired map is defined, while #{\det K=0}.}
